# Security baseline

## Secrets
- Never put secrets, API keys, tokens, passwords or private keys in code, tests, CI configuration, commit messages or logs.
- Read credentials from the environment or the CI secret store at runtime. If a change needs a new secret, name the variable and stop; do not invent placeholder values that look real.

## External input
- Treat every byte from the network, files, environment variables and command-line arguments as untrusted.
- Validate length, range and format before use. Reject malformed input with an explicit error instead of guessing or repairing it.

## Network calls and dependencies
- Do not add network calls (sockets, HTTP clients, DNS lookups, outbound connections from scripts or CI) without review.
  - Agent shells deny curl, wget and netcat, and inline scripts that make network calls: `python -c` code that uses urllib, requests or socket; `node -e` / `--eval` / `-p` / `--print`, `bun -e` / `--eval`, `deno eval` and `deno repl --eval` code that uses fetch, http, https, net, tls, dgram, undici, WebSocket or a Deno network API (`Deno.connect`, `Deno.listen`, `Deno.resolveDns`, ...); `perl -e` / `-E` and `ruby -e` code (or `-M` / `-r` modules) that uses LWP, HTTP::Tiny, IO::Socket, Net::HTTP, Net::FTP, open-uri, Socket or URI; and inline code of any of these that names curl, wget or netcat.
  - They also deny `deno` with a network permission (`--allow-net` / `-N`, `--allow-import` / `-I`, `--allow-all` / `-A`, in any subcommand) and `deno repl --eval-file`, which runs files with every permission. A local `deno run ./script.ts` without those permissions is allowed, as are `deno fmt`, `deno lint`, `deno test` and `deno check` of local files.
  - `xargs` running `gh`, `git` or `deno` is denied whatever its other arguments (`echo repos/x/y | xargs gh api`, `xargs -I{} gh run view {}`, `xargs git fetch`, `xargs deno run`), since its input supplies arguments the hook cannot see; any other program run by `xargs` is checked as if it had one more unknown argument, so `xargs grep` and `xargs rm -f build/*.o` behave as without `xargs`.
- Do not add libraries, packages, toolchains, GitHub Actions or downloaded binaries without review. Prefer what the distribution's package manager already provides.
- New third-party code always enters this repository through a human-reviewed pull request, never from an agent shell.
- New outside code (dependencies, repositories, remotes) needs human review before it enters this repository. Agent shells enforce this in `.cursor/hooks/shell_policy.py` and deny:
  - `git clone`, `git submodule add`, `git remote add` and `git archive --remote` (any remote, including `origin`), always.
  - `git ls-remote` against anything other than `origin`; bare `git ls-remote` and `git ls-remote origin` (with `--heads` or `--tags`) are allowed.
  - `git fetch`, `git pull` and `git remote update` from any remote other than `origin`: another remote name, a URL (`https://`, `git@`, `ssh://`, `file://`) or a local path, and `--all`. With no remote or with `origin` they are allowed, also after `git -C <dir>` and harmless `-c key=value` such as `core.quotepath=false` or `protocol.version=2`.
  - Any git command whose config is overridden so that `origin`, a submodule or a subcommand could point elsewhere: `-c` or `--config-env` on a `remote.*`, `url.*` (`insteadOf`, `pushInsteadOf`), `include.*`, `includeIf.*`, `alias.*`, `submodule.*.url` or `submodule.*.update` key (other `submodule.*` keys such as `submodule.recurse` are allowed); `GIT_CONFIG_KEY_<n>` or `GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS` naming such a key; and a moved config file (`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, `GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM`, `GIT_CONFIG`, or `HOME` / `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` for git). This covers `VAR=... git`, `env VAR=... git`, nested `bash -c`, `export`, and standalone assignments; the `GIT_CONFIG_*` variables are denied in front of any command.
  - Retargeting remotes, submodules and aliases: `git remote set-url` (including `--add` and `--push`), `git submodule set-url`, `git remote rename` onto `origin`, and every `git config` write to a `remote.*`, `url.*`, `submodule.*`, `alias.*`, `include.*` or `includeIf.*` key or section: setting a value, `--add`, `--replace-all`, `--unset`, `--unset-all`, `--rename-section` (to or from such a section), `--remove-section`, and `git config set` / `unset` / `rename-section` / `remove-section`, with or without `--file` / `-f`, `--global`, `--system`, `--local`, `--worktree` or `--blob`. git accepts any unique prefix of a long option, so the policy classifies on the key and on whether the command writes, not on spelling: `--fil`, `--glo`, `--glob`, `--repl` and `--ad` count as `--file`, `--global`, `--replace-all` and `--add`. Also denied: any write to `.gitmodules` through a file option, for every key; `git config --edit`; and any `git config` whose read or write form cannot be determined, such as an unknown option, an ambiguous prefix (`--fi` could be `--file` or `--fixed-value`, `--a` could be `--add`, `--all` or `--append`), a key, section or file name that the shell expands (`$KEY`, backticks, `{a,b}`, a glob), or a subcommand word that an option before it takes as its value. Reads such as `git config --get remote.origin.url`, `git config -l`, `git config --get-regexp alias`, `git config -f .gitmodules --get-regexp url` and `git remote get-url origin` are allowed, as are writes to other keys (`git config user.name x`, `git config --glo core.autocrlf false`), `git submodule status`, `git submodule update --init` and `git submodule sync`.
  - Package installs: `pip install` (also `python -m pip install`), `npm install` / `npm i` / `npm add`, `yarn add`, `pnpm add`, `deno add`, `go get` and `cargo add`, and `pnpm install` / `pnpm i` / `yarn install` / `deno install` with a package name. `npm ci`, `npm test`, `go build`, `cargo build`, and `pnpm install` / `yarn install` / `deno install` without a package name are allowed.
  - Remote modules for deno: an `https://`, `http://`, `npm:`, `jsr:` or `data:` specifier anywhere on a `deno` command line (`deno run https://...`, `deno https://...`, `deno run npm:cowsay`, `--import-map=https://...`, `deno repl --eval-file=https://...`), and `deno eval` / `deno repl --eval` code that imports an `npm:` or `jsr:` specifier or an `http(s)://` URL.
  - GitHub CLI: `gh repo clone`, `gh gist clone`, `gh release download` (any repository, including this one), `gh extension install` and `gh repo fork --clone` / `--remote`, always. `gh alias` with any subcommand (`set`, `import`, `delete`, `list`), also after `-R` or other flags, since an alias can hide any of these or a shell command. Because gh runs a user alias or an extension for a word it does not know, and adds aliases one word below any command group (`gh run x`), every word where gh expects a subcommand must be a gh 2.102 command or one of its cobra aliases; other words are denied (`gh co`, `gh pr mine`, `gh run x`), as is an option other than `-R` / `--repo` before the subcommand (`gh pr --json number list`), since it can hide which word is the command. Use the full command instead, e.g. `gh pr checkout 4`. Any `gh` command aimed at a repository other than `quinndarling21/sigrelay` through `-R` / `--repo` (`OWNER/REPO`, `HOST/OWNER/REPO` or a URL) or `GH_REPO`, and any `gh` command with `GH_HOST` other than `github.com`. `gh pr checkout` / `gh pr diff` given another repository's URL. `gh api` on a `repos/<owner>/<repo>` path of another repository (with or without a leading `/`, or as an `https://api.github.com` URL), on `repositories/<id>`, on another host (`--hostname` or a URL), and `gh api graphql` always, since a query can reach any repository. Everything against this repository is allowed: `gh pr ...`, `gh run list` / `view` / `download`, `gh release list` / `view`, and `gh api repos/quinndarling21/sigrelay/...` or `repos/{owner}/{repo}/...`, also with `-R quinndarling21/sigrelay`.
- When an agent shell blocks a command, do not work around it; report what you wanted to add and why.

## Logging
- Use structured logging with explicit levels (error, warn, info, debug). Every message carries an event name and key-value fields, not free-form string concatenation.
- Never log full SIP or Diameter message payloads.
- Never log subscriber identifiers (IMSI, MSISDN, SIP URIs or anything derived from them) at info level or above. When an identifier is needed for debugging, log it at debug level only, truncated or hashed.
- Never log secrets, session keys or authentication material at any level.
